WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:02.854
[SOUND] How many of you
have found yourself in

00:00:02.854 --> 00:00:07.726
the situation where you're talking
to someone from another country, or

00:00:07.726 --> 00:00:11.496
from another culture,
even from another state or city,

00:00:11.496 --> 00:00:16.866
that you don't quite get what they're
saying and you're just kind of confused?

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:18.562
Cameron's raising his hands.

00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:22.320
You're kind of clueless and sometimes you
just, you don't know what's going on.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.410
Well, we're here to talk to you today
about cross-cultural communication.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.180
And we think it's a very important
topic to the four of us and

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.430
we hope it's valuable to you as well.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.541
So, first thing we want to show you is
cross-cultural exchanges everywhere.

00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:40.637
And some put of a little bit funny
picture there but it show you that even

00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:45.910
simple places like a market, remote place
in China that it still exists as well.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.514
But so the point here is, did you know
that translation services is actually

00:00:49.514 --> 00:00:52.236
a $40 billion industry and
it's growing 8% a year?

00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:56.060
Did you know that 40% of your classmates
here are from another country?

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.790
Did you know that also terrorism and
cross-border commerce and

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.340
all the things required nuanced
to cultural understanding?

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.600
That's sometimes actually
beyond the simple language.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:13.318
And what I'll show you here is a picture
of George W with King Abdullah from Saudi.

00:01:13.318 --> 00:01:15.125
You might find that strange but

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:19.160
it's actually in their customs to
hold hands while two mens talk.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.846
It just goes to show that it is really
a powerful aspect of communication

00:01:24.846 --> 00:01:29.606
that all this cultural differences
underline what we say.

00:01:29.606 --> 00:01:33.067
So, the things we also want to point
out is we haven't had a session on

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:35.804
cross-cultural communication
from this class so,

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:38.904
I think it is under-appreciated
in some sense.

00:01:38.904 --> 00:01:42.280
And sometimes, we all speak English
here obviously have GSB but

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.030
it really delight some of
the underlying differences that people

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.360
don't really express from
a purely linguistic perspective.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.270
And the second last point is really some
of these things are very hard to know

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.960
because we are talking to
someone from another culture.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.450
They don't really give you a lot of
feedback or we lose attention on them.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.310
They don't really give you, hey, sorry,

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:07.390
I didn't understand, that's very rarely
happens unless you guys are really close.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:12.350
So, but the good news for
all of you guys is we are here to help.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.885
So today,
we are going to talk to you guys about

00:02:14.885 --> 00:02:18.055
some of our personal stories from
the past, what we experienced,

00:02:18.055 --> 00:02:23.265
and hopefully bring to you what
JD terms conscious incompetence.

00:02:23.265 --> 00:02:25.755
So, you're aware at least when you're
talking to someone from another culture

00:02:25.755 --> 00:02:27.845
that some things are not going across.

00:02:27.845 --> 00:02:30.625
And lastly, we're going to offer
you some advice and tips so

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:36.085
you guys have a sense of, one maybe small
things we can do to be very effective.

00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:39.849
So with that, I'm going to pass off to
Funso who's going to talk to you about

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:42.371
some of the verbal
communication challenges.

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:46.990
&gt;&gt; Yes, so, what we want you to take
away from this class is to stay lit.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:48.846
We want you to listen, inquire and

00:02:48.846 --> 00:02:53.461
test things out whenever you're talking to
people who are from different cultures.

00:02:53.461 --> 00:02:54.765
But before we even do that,

00:02:54.765 --> 00:02:58.020
what are some things that might
get into the way of that?

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.250
Well, in Fred Jandt's book,
An Introduction to

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.750
Intercultural Communication, there
are three things that we consider to be

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.500
barriers to communication when it
comes to the aspect of listening.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:12.322
The first one is anxiety and
how many of us have traveled the world and

00:03:12.322 --> 00:03:15.050
got into a new country,
we're not really sure of the norms are,

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.480
we're not really sure of the culture is or
how to shake hands or do those things.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.580
Anxiety is the number one
reason that people impacted and

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.370
affects people's communication
across cultures.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:29.460
A second one is judging other
groups by your own culture.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.540
I mean, this may seem common sense,
but you might think that everyone does

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.480
the same things or acts the same way that
you do when you go into a new environment.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.050
And that's another reason why people
have found it really challenging to

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.900
communicate with people in other cultures.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.250
A third reason is,
is the same language different meaning.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.640
I mean Jeff speaks English,
I speak English.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.650
Ashton, the other cohort, speaks English.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.817
But I'm speaking
Nigerian/American-English,

00:03:52.817 --> 00:03:56.190
she's speaking British-English, and
Ash is speaking Australian-English.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:57.550
We're all speaking the same language but

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.300
there are different words
that mean different things.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.430
And it's important to be very aware that
you might be using the same words but

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.130
to understand that there might be
deeper meaning in those words.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:13.070
And so, to bring it back to the GSB,
what are some common mishaps that happen?

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:16.139
I mean, how many of us have been in
a class where someone has said something

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:17.192
go ahead and break a leg?

00:04:17.192 --> 00:04:20.379
I remember the first time I came to
the US and someone told me to do that,

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:22.552
I was really concerned and
backed away slowly.

00:04:22.552 --> 00:04:23.852
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:04:23.852 --> 00:04:27.420
that's not what that means an incur event
maybe you're about to go on a show.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.070
But these are their idiomatic
expressions such as this that

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.940
if your coming from another country you
may not be very familiar what that means.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.690
And it's important to recognize that
you may have to explain that to

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:38.800
another person.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.860
Another expression is references.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.710
A professor in class asks someone
a question, to which no one answered.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:50.470
And she proceeds to say, Bueller, Bueller,
Bueller, who knows what I'm talking about?

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.504
This is a reference to
Ferris Bueller's Day Off.

00:04:53.504 --> 00:04:56.884
Now, in a class like this where,
the class is 40% international,

00:04:56.884 --> 00:04:58.930
you may not know what that means.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.030
And you've lost 40% of your class,
and they are lost,

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.350
they've missed the entire message of
what you're trying to get across.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.216
We're very guilty of this, especially at
the GSB, and it's very important to be

00:05:09.216 --> 00:05:12.353
aware that when you make movie or
TV or even sports references, and

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:15.344
not everyone's going to understand
what you're referring to.

00:05:15.344 --> 00:05:17.220
The third one are nonexistent words.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:21.032
And what I mean by that is for
example if you're speaking Spanish,

00:05:21.032 --> 00:05:25.603
there's a word that's using Spanish,
but the word does not exist in English.

00:05:25.603 --> 00:05:29.329
Those are very difficult to transfer
from one language to the other, and

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:33.175
if you find a word that's very close,
some of that meaning is also lost so,

00:05:33.175 --> 00:05:35.150
being very aware of what that means.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.830
So that's it for verbal communication,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:41.022
remember to stay lit and
I'll pass it on to Willem.

00:05:43.847 --> 00:05:47.621
&gt;&gt; So, when I was five years old,
my family moved from the US to Mexico,

00:05:47.621 --> 00:05:50.030
we made wonderful Mexican friends there.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:55.040
They taught us local customs,
traditions, and then, gestures as well.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.115
Some of these gestures included thank you,
and

00:05:58.115 --> 00:06:02.520
[FOREIGN] which is also kind of a lot,
or it can also mean it's crowded here.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.830
So then, about a year and a half later,
we picked up and moved to Argentina.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:12.100
And this is a picture of a very
famous place in Argentina,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.585
it's also one of the worst intersections
you can imagine for traffic.

00:06:15.585 --> 00:06:18.723
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Here lanes mean effectively nothing,

00:06:18.723 --> 00:06:21.588
direction of travel is
kind of a suggestion.

00:06:21.588 --> 00:06:22.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.270
one day my father was driving my
brother and I along this road and

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:31.430
by some weird fluke some guy stopped
to let us merge in front of him.

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.650
Father who was confused but grateful and

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:39.450
gestures what he believed to kind of thank
you a lot and it's also crowded here.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.620
So, he thinks this is like
the perfectly efficient use of gesture.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:45.993
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And from one second to the next,

00:06:45.993 --> 00:06:50.531
this guy goes from this calm,
relaxed guy letting us in front of him,

00:06:50.531 --> 00:06:53.663
to just kind of maniacal
road raging monkey man,

00:06:53.663 --> 00:06:58.230
who jumps out of his car beating on
the hood and the windows of our car.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So, at this point,

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:04.608
I'm really thankful that when my father
steps out of the car his physical

00:07:04.608 --> 00:07:07.685
stature alone is enough to
kind of resolve this issue.

00:07:07.685 --> 00:07:10.552
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But later that night,

00:07:10.552 --> 00:07:16.234
we learned that to Chileans,
of whom there are many in Argentina,

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:21.814
the Mexican [FOREIGN] is actually
[FOREIGN] which is frequently used

00:07:21.814 --> 00:07:27.025
as [FOREIGN] or directly
translated as your mother's shell.

00:07:27.025 --> 00:07:27.537
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:07:27.537 --> 00:07:30.992
I will let you all think
about the details there.

00:07:30.992 --> 00:07:35.678
But this was an example of a dangerous
assumption you don't want to

00:07:35.678 --> 00:07:38.710
make about nonverbal communication.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:44.100
So, while it can be an extremely
effective and useful tool,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.630
I urge you all to be both aware and

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:54.850
respectful of not only the obvious
differences in kind of personal space or

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:00.638
gestures although, I guess not that
obvious to my father or eye contact.

00:08:00.638 --> 00:08:02.580
But also kind of more subtle and

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.085
nuance differences in the cultural
understanding of time,

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:10.890
kind of a pace of activity, and the use
of silence, and also the use of touch.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.735
So, with that,
I'm going to turn it over to George.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:14.853
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Willem.

00:08:14.853 --> 00:08:17.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That was the Mexican thank you,

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:21.890
not a suggestion about Willem's mother,
who I'm sure is a wonderful lady.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:27.160
Has anyone in here ever used a translator,
anyone?

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.150
It's a very interesting and
challenging experience.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:33.890
I was in the military and
I served in the Middle East and then,

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:38.410
in Southeast Asia, and a lot of partners
that we worked with to mentor and

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:40.900
conduct their own missions
didn't speak English.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:43.780
They either spoke,
a language of Afghanistan, or Tagalog,

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:48.250
which is a language in
the southeastern Philippines.

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.290
So, I've often found myself
dealing with translators.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:53.650
And so, the tools that I'm going to
share with you today are things that I

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:57.150
learned the hard way through a lot of
sweat, and tears, and embarrassment.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.840
One of those that I'd like to highlight
is that humor doesn't translate

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.310
nearly as well, and

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.930
through another language, particularly
through the filter of a translator.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:10.060
If you can imagine the downside of really
offending someone in another language

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:14.360
is huge, and trying to get a small chuckle
out of someone isn't even worth the risk,

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.760
my advice to you, don't try and
make a joke through a translator.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.924
Something that I am very guilty of,
is when I would have meetings,

00:09:20.924 --> 00:09:22.507
I would speak to my translator and

00:09:22.507 --> 00:09:26.195
I would lose rapport with the audience
that I was actually trying to speak to.

00:09:26.195 --> 00:09:30.069
So, maintain a conversation and
eye contact with one individual as he or

00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:32.750
she was meant to translate
to the audience.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.010
And I completely isolated and

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.790
marginalized this person because
I focus on the translator.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.140
So, I encourage you, if you're
ever using a translator, in fact,

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:44.060
just be mindful to maintain rapport and
body language and

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.820
eye contact with your intended audience,
not your translator.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:52.400
Something that to me seems
intuitive as I looked back and

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.100
having made these mistakes,
is something about preparation.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.550
Sit down and
present with your translator, or

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.170
share your slides with your translator
prior to the actual event so

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:07.320
that they can proofread and help you out
and actually you own your own message.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.180
Something that's unique about translators
is that you think you have your own

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.200
microphone.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:15.730
But what the audience actually hears is
the translator's message, not your own.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.880
So, if you're going to own your message,
make sure that you

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.670
craft it exactly how you want it and
practice with your translator.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:29.260
There's a number of other things that I
have stories I would share with you, but

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:30.480
we're running out of time.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.502
One of those is around jargon
in colloquial phrases.

00:10:33.502 --> 00:10:37.481
I think we've learned a lot about that
this morning, through Willem's gestures

00:10:37.481 --> 00:10:40.900
as well as the sports metaphors but
I just want to reiterate it.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.100
Don't try and make a colloquial
phrase through a translator,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.230
because it just isn't going to work well.

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:50.020
And so, when it comes to translators and
then, wrapping it up,

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:54.060
what we really want you guys to take away
is this cute little acronym we devised to

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:58.580
stay LIT, really do listen,
inquire, and test.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:02.500
Anytime you're going to be
communicating cross-culturally.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:06.200
And lastly, we want to serve as resources
for all of you going forward so,

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.660
I've listed our contact
information behind us and

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.580
some of the geographies that
we've exposed ourselves to.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.972
So, if you ever find yourself either
throughout your career at the GSB or

00:11:14.972 --> 00:11:18.474
after business school going to one of
these geographies, drop us a note and

00:11:18.474 --> 00:11:22.201
we can give you some of the stories and
lessons that we've learned the hard way so

00:11:22.201 --> 00:11:24.618
that you don't make the same
mistakes that we did.

00:11:24.618 --> 00:11:29.069
[MUSIC]

